To understand not just the final result but the entire learning process, I wrote a custom K-Means function that logs every iteration. This way, I can track how both cluster assignments and centroid positions evolve over time. Below is the implementation, which runs for a maximum of 100 iterations and stores the full trace needed to build step-by-step plots.

kmeans_custom_trace &lt;- function(data, k, max_iter = 100, seed = 123){
  set.seed(seed)
  n         &lt;- nrow(data)
  centroids &lt;- data[sample(1:n, k), ]                # random initial centroids
  trace_lst &lt;- list()

  for(i in seq_len(max_iter)){
    # ----- assignment step -----
    assign_vec &lt;- apply(data, 1, function(pt){
      which.min(colSums((t(centroids) - pt)^2))
    })

    # ----- update step -----
    new_centroids &lt;- matrix(NA, nrow = k, ncol = ncol(data))
    for(c in seq_len(k)){
      pts &lt;- data[assign_vec == c, , drop = FALSE]
      new_centroids[c, ] &lt;- if(nrow(pts) &gt; 0) colMeans(pts) else centroids[c, ]
    }

    # ----- store trace -----
    trace_lst[[i]] &lt;- list(iteration = i,
                           clusters   = assign_vec,
                           centroids  = as.data.frame(new_centroids))

    centroids &lt;- new_centroids
  }
  trace_lst
}
